Implementing multiple-choice dialogue in a text-based Java game is a common requirement. The best approach depends on the complexity of your game.

Here are the three most effective methods, ranging from simple to robust.

### Method 1: The "Switch-Case" Approach (Best for Simple Games)
This is the most straightforward method. You store the dialogue text and the options in a single object, and use a `switch` statement to handle the user's input.

**Pros:** Very easy to read and debug.
**Cons:** Can become messy if you have hundreds of dialogue lines.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

class DialogueNode {
    String text;
    DialogueNode[] options;
    int optionCount;

    public DialogueNode(String text, DialogueNode[] options) {
        this.text = text;
        this.options = options;
        this.optionCount = options.length;
    }

    public void display(Scanner scanner) {
        System.out.println("\n" + text);
        for (int i = 0; i < optionCount; i++) {
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + options[i].text);
        }
        System.out.print("Enter choice: ");
        int choice = scanner.nextInt();

        // Handle the choice
        switch (choice) {
            case 1:
                options[0].display(scanner);
                break;
            case 2:
                options[1].display(scanner);
                break;
            case 3:
                options[2].display(scanner);
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid choice.");
                display(scanner); // Retry
                break;
        }
    }
}

// Example Usage
